const description = "Fridge warm but freezer still cold? It's almost always the evaporator fan, defrost system, or damper. What each one means, safe checks, and when to call.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my fridge not cooling but the freezer is still cold?",
    a: "On almost every modern refrigerator, all the cold air is made in one place: the evaporator coil behind the freezer wall. A small fan blows that cold air up into the fresh-food compartment. When the freezer stays cold but the fridge turns warm, the cold is still being made, it just isn't reaching the top. The three usual causes are a failed evaporator fan motor, a stuck or frosted-over defrost system, or a broken air damper. All three are common between years 5 and 12, and repair typically runs $260 to $460."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I know if it's the evaporator fan?",
    a: "Open the freezer and listen with the door switch held in (or the light pressed). A healthy evaporator fan makes a steady whir. If the freezer is cold but silent, the fan motor has likely failed and warm air is no longer being pushed to the fridge side. This is a safe thing for a homeowner to check. Actually replacing the motor means pulling the freezer back panel, which is where our techs take over."
  },
  {
    q: "Could it be too much frost on the coil?",
    a: "Yes. Frost-free refrigerators run an automatic defrost cycle every several hours. When the defrost heater, timer, or termination thermostat quits, frost slowly packs the evaporator coil solid, air can't move through it, and the fridge warms up over a week or two while the freezer holds on longer. If you pull the freezer back panel and see a solid wall of ice on the coil, that's the defrost system, not the fan."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a fridge that's warm but freezer cold worth repairing?",
    a: "Usually yes. An evaporator fan, damper, or defrost part is a $260 to $460 repair, and none of it touches the sealed refrigerant system. On a fridge under year 12 that math almost always favors repair over a $1,500-plus replacement. We give you the number before any work starts. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What can I safely try before calling?",
    a: "Four things, all safe: make sure the fridge and freezer vents inside aren't blocked by stacked food; check that the temperature dials weren't bumped; listen for the evaporator fan in the freezer; and look for a solid ice buildup on the freezer back wall. If food isn't blocking vents and the fan is silent or the coil is iced, it's a component failure and it's time for a tech. Do not attempt to take apart the sealed system or the compressor."
  }
];

